# 🚨 MANDATORY: Retrieve BEFORE editing (ZERO TOLERANCE)

**`{{ORG_ALIAS}}` (sandbox) is the source of truth — the local repo is NOT.** Teammates and admins deploy directly to the org, so files on disk can be hours/days/weeks stale. **Before modifying ANY metadata you plan to deploy — Apex, LWC, Aura, OmniStudio, objects/fields, validation rules, web links, layouts, FlexiPages, permission sets, flows, custom metadata, etc. — you MUST retrieve the current version from `{{ORG_ALIAS}}` first.**

Editing a stale file and then deploying **silently overwrites the org's newer state**. This step is non-negotiable and precedes every other rule (syntax validation, schema lookup, styling, cache-bust, doc workflow — all of them assume you retrieved first).

> Full `sf` retrieve flag surface: [`sf-cli-commands.mdc`](./sf-cli-commands.mdc). This rule is the *mandate*; that rule is the *reference*.

## Step 1 — Map the file path to the metadata type + API name

| Path under `force-app/main/default/` | Metadata type | API name |
|---|---|---|
| `classes/<Name>.cls` | `ApexClass` | `<Name>` |
| `triggers/<Name>.trigger` | `ApexTrigger` | `<Name>` |
| `lwc/<bundle>/...` | `LightningComponentBundle` | `<bundle>` |
| `aura/<bundle>/...` | `AuraDefinitionBundle` | `<bundle>` |
| `omniScripts/<Type_SubType_Lang_Version>.os-meta.xml` | `OmniScript` | `<Type_SubType_Lang_Version>` |
| `omniIntegrationProcedures/<Name>.oip-meta.xml` | `OmniIntegrationProcedure` | `<Name>` |
| `omniDataTransforms/<Name>.rpt-meta.xml` | `OmniDataTransform` | `<Name>` |
| `objects/<SObj>/fields/<F>.field-meta.xml` | `CustomField` | `<SObj>.<F>` |
| `objects/<SObj>/webLinks/<W>.webLink-meta.xml` | `WebLink` | `<SObj>.<W>` |
| `objects/<SObj>/validationRules/<R>.validationRule-meta.xml` | `ValidationRule` | `<SObj>.<R>` |
| `objects/<SObj>/recordTypes/<RT>.recordType-meta.xml` | `RecordType` | `<SObj>.<RT>` |
| `objects/<SObj>/listViews/<L>.listView-meta.xml` | `ListView` | `<SObj>.<L>` |
| `objects/<SObj>/<SObj>.object-meta.xml` | `CustomObject` | `<SObj>` |
| `flexipages/<Name>.flexipage-meta.xml` | `FlexiPage` | `<Name>` |
| `layouts/<SObj>-<Layout>.layout-meta.xml` | `Layout` | `<SObj>-<Layout>` |
| `permissionsets/<Name>.permissionset-meta.xml` | `PermissionSet` | `<Name>` |
| `flows/<Name>.flow-meta.xml` | `Flow` | `<Name>` |
| `customMetadata/<Type>.<Record>.md-meta.xml` | `CustomMetadata` | `<Type>.<Record>` |
| `staticresources/<Name>.resource-meta.xml` | `StaticResource` | `<Name>` |

For multi-file bundles (LWC / Aura / OmniScript), the API name is the **bundle / parent name**, not the inner file. There is no way to retrieve a single `.js` inside an LWC, Aura, or OmniScript — you get the whole bundle.

## Step 2 — Run the retrieve

```bash
# Single component (preferred — fast, precise)
sf project retrieve start --metadata <Type>:<ApiName> -o {{ORG_ALIAS}}

# Multiple related components — use a scoped manifest
sf project retrieve start --manifest manifest/<feature>.xml -o {{ORG_ALIAS}}
```

If the retrieve reports `nothing retrieved`, the component may no longer exist in the org. **Stop and confirm with the user** before editing or recreating it.

## Step 3 — Compare, then edit

Re-read the file after the retrieve completes. If git shows the retrieve changed it, your local copy was stale — review the new contents before applying your edit so you don't reintroduce removed code.

**Read the diff for deletions, not just additions.** Two properties of `sf project retrieve start` make a retrieve look successful while leaving the tree wrong, and both are invisible if you only check that the file changed:

**Retrieve is add-and-overwrite; it never deletes.** A component deleted in the org leaves its local file untouched, so the deletion is absent from the diff entirely. The stale file then keeps serving schema the org no longer has — and if you deploy it back, you *recreate* the component someone deliberately removed. A file count at or above the previous baseline is therefore not evidence of correctness; it is equally the signature of an undetected deletion. When you retrieve a wildcard or a whole type, reconcile the org's `list metadata` fullNames against the component names on disk rather than assuming a superset is fine.

**A container and its child type decompose into the same file, and the later retrieve wins.** Retrieving a child type alone rewrites the shared file with only that child's members and silently drops everything the container contributed — several live rules can vanish from one file while the command reports success. This bites hardest with the rule-container types (sharing rules, workflow, matching/assignment/auto-response/escalation rules, custom labels) whose children are separately addressable. Two consequences for the edit loop:

- **Retrieve the container, not the child**, when you need one child of it. Addressing the container gets you the union; addressing the child can cost you the rest.
- **If you did retrieve a child type, re-retrieve its container afterwards** and confirm the file is back to the union before you edit or deploy it.

```bash
# After retrieving any child type, check whether the shared file lost members.
git diff --stat -- force-app/main/default/objects/
git diff -- <the-shared-file>   # deletions here are the tell
```

## Step 4 — Recheck org freshness before deploy

If analysis/review/implementation took time, compare the org component state again immediately before deployment **without retrieving over the edited working files** — query each component's `LastModifiedDate` and `LastModifiedById`, or retrieve into a throwaway `--target-metadata-dir`:

```bash
sf data query -o {{ORG_ALIAS}} --use-tooling-api \
  --query "SELECT Name, LastModifiedDate, LastModifiedBy.Name FROM ApexClass WHERE Name IN ('<Class1>','<Class2>')"
```

That Tooling-API form works for ApexClass, ApexTrigger, CustomField, CustomObject, Layout, FlexiPage, Flow, PermissionSet, ValidationRule, RecordType, WebLink, and the LWC/Aura bundles. It does **not** work for the Omni types — `OmniProcess` and `OmniDataTransform` are not Tooling objects and return `sObject type ... is not supported`. Query those on the standard data API instead, and on a Vlocity CMT org use `vlocity_cmt__OmniScript__c` / `vlocity_cmt__DRBundle__c`, since the standard objects are empty there.

Compare against the timestamps captured at Step 2. If nothing moved, deploy promptly — the shorter the gap between this check and the deploy, the smaller the overwrite race. If a teammate or admin changed an intended component after Step 2, STOP: merge their change, revalidate and re-test, then rerun the Gate B critics against the new revision. `--ignore-conflicts` never authorizes overwriting newer org work.

## Scope

- **Default org alias is `{{ORG_ALIAS}}`** (sandbox). Every `sf` command in this repo assumes `-o {{ORG_ALIAS}}`. Confirm with `sf org list --all`.
- This is about **single-file or scoped** retrieves in the normal edit loop. For a full-org refresh, follow [`docs/org-mirror/README.md`](../../docs/org-mirror/README.md) — never a single org-wide `*` retrieve, which exceeds the per-call cap on any non-trivial org.
- Schema TOON files under `config/schema/` are refreshed separately via `python3 scripts/schemapy/auto_generate_schema.py`, not by this flow. See [`salesforce-schema-validation.mdc`](./salesforce-schema-validation.mdc).

## When NOT to retrieve

- Files outside `force-app/main/default/` (docs, scripts, manifests, schema TOONs).
- Read-only inspection — only required before **edits**.
- Brand-new components you are creating from scratch (nothing to retrieve).
